WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.600
Roland Emmerich's Midway is a historical war
movie that has audiences shaking their heads,

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.400
with plenty of things that just seem too insane
to be true.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.520
But sometimes reality really is stranger than
fiction.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.280
Here are the things in Midway you won't believe
were true.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:16.900
"This is actually what happened."

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.200
Most of Midway's characters are based on real
people, including Nick Jonas's Bruno Gaido.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.650
Remember that unlikely scene where the USS
Enterprise is raided by Japanese bombers,

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.000
only to have them all miss... except for one,
badly damaged, about to crash into the ship?

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.830
Gaido hops into a nearby dive bomber and starts
shooting, only to send the bomber careening

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.860
away at the last minute, at the same time
the plane he's in takes a direct hit.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.300
Surely Hollywood drama, right?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.680
It's absolutely true.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.180
Gaido really did take a running leap into
a parked plane, then opened fire on a Japanese

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.340
bomber intent on crashing into the Enterprise,
just like the movie depicts.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.960
The Japanese bomber really did clip Gaido's
plane, slicing off the tail and sending it

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.160
skidding across the deck.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.800
"Too much excitement for you?"

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.560
There is a tad bit of creative license that
was taken.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.540
In the aftermath of the real incident, Gaido
extinguished a gasoline fire and then disappeared.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.060
He had been afraid he was going to get reprimanded
for leaving his watch position, but instead,

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:17.120
he was found, taken in front of Admiral Halsey,
and promoted on the spot just like the film

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:17.620
shows.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.260
Even the bravest military is going to fail
without reliable weapons and equipment, and

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.200
that makes it all the more shocking that Midway
suggests the Pacific front was fighting with

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.520
torpedoes that didn't exactly work.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.420
The film makes several mentions of torpedoes
not being tested or reliable, and even showing

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.740
one scoring a direct hit only to break apart
and sink.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.900
Unlikely-sounding?

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:38.600
Yes.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.240
True?

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.060
Also yes.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.259
In real life, the Mark 14 Torpedo was reportedly
pretty much useless, with a tendency to just...

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.340
not explode after colliding with its target.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.120
And the military knew it, sending the torpedoes
into combat even though they had around a

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.480
50 percent failure rate.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:59.300
Just a few months after Pearl Harbor, that
failure rate had increased to around 80 percent.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.840
Midway is a visually stunning movie that puts
audiences right in the cockpit of World War

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:05.760
II dive-bombers.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:09.759
It seems insane that pilots would literally
dive through a rain of fire to try and sink

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.540
massive ships but they did.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.040
The Douglas SBD Dauntless dive-bomber was
the plane known as "Slow But Deadly."

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.260
They were flying at a time when aircraft were
still pretty bare-bones, and in the mid-1930s,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.470
both the Navy and the Marines were beginning
to appreciate the death-defying practice of

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.700
dive-bombing as the best way to accurately
aim, and they built aircraft suited to the

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.200
task.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.740
The Dauntless had things like split flaps
and dive brakes, and allowed pilots to dive

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.140
at an 80-degree angle at speeds nearing 300
mph.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.660
They turned the tide at Midway, when taking
advantage of a sky momentarily clear of Japanese

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.220
counter-attacks Commander Clarence McClusky
led just a handful of dive-bombers against

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.640
the Japanese fleet, mortally damaging three
of the four carriers in around five minutes.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.040
The Doolittle Raid is treated as something
of an aside in Midway, a daring mission happening

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.220
alongside the main action.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.540
We see Jimmy Doolittle and his men heading
out to launch some serious retaliation on

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.600
Tokyo, with no guarantees that they're carrying
enough fuel to get them to safety.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.650
Aaron Eckhart's Doolittle makes it into Japanese-held
China and meets up with allies happy to see

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.380
the Americans who struck a blow to their oppressors.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.960
Again, absolutely true.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.930
The mission was reportedly so secret that
President Franklin Roosevelt wasn't even initially

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:21.980
informed about it.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.820
The plan was to take off around 300 miles
from Japan's shores, but as in the film they

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.680
were forced to take off much earlier.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:32.940
The fleet was spotted when they were 700 miles
out, and the early launch may have saved the

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.440
mission.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.680
Japanese commanders knew the short range of
American fighters and weren't expecting a

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.180
long-range launch and weren't ready for it.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.700
It's an odd little detail, at first.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.260
Midway's Admiral Halsey, played by Dennis
Quaid, complains of a rash that quickly gets

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.440
worse.Halsey is ordered to step down and head
to the hospital.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.840
Even those with a passing knowledge of World
War II's Pacific Theater have heard of Rear

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.900
Admiral William F. "Bull" Halsey, so... what
gives?

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.640
Was he really sidelined by a rash?

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:00.440
Yes.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.000
According to the US Naval Institute, it was
just one of a handful of incidents where illness

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:05.960
impacted command.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.600
That rash was a severe case of psoriasis,
and it was so bad it was interfering with

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:12.280
his ability to make decisions.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.940
The biggest change the movie made was making
it a symptom of shingles, which is contagious,

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.900
maybe making his absence seem more justifiable
to an audience.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.720
Wouldn't want to put other people at risk
on the battlefield, after all.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:28.300
When audiences see the USS Yorktown after
the Battle of Coral Sea, she's in a dismal

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:28.800
state.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:33.040
She's in dry dock, gaping holes in her deck,
with crews that are dwarfed by the sheer size

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.640
of the damage they're trying to fix.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.920
But in the film, she's back on the water and
sailing in to save the day just hours afterwards.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:39.900
Unlikely?

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:40.640
Yes.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:41.640
But also true.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.080
According to the US Navy, the Yorktown returned
to Pearl Harbor with an open hull, leaking

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.800
oil, and sporting enough damage that it was
estimated it would be 90 days before she was

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.930
operational again.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:57.029
A 551-pound bomb had sliced through the deck
and detonated 50 feet into the ship.

00:04:57.029 --> 00:05:01.629
Her hull was cracked, elevators non-functional,
and she had left 10 miles of oil and fuel

00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:02.280
in her wake.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.820
When she returned to Pearl Harbor, plans for
Midway were already underway.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.820
Admiral Nimitz gave them three days to get
the Yorktown back in the water ... and they

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:10.320
did it.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.040
Around 1,400 workers labored for 72 hours
straight on repairs that were so intensive

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.569
the island was subjected to a series of blackouts
to funnel electricity to the repair docks.

00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:26.699
In Midway, American planes have a crucial
advantage: time, chaos, and confusion stemming

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.550
from an armaments change-over by the Japanese
military.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.620
And that happened.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.240
According to the Smithsonian, the Japanese
air fleet was originally equipped with torpedoes,

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.180
geared for fighting American carriers.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.889
Vice Admiral Chuichi Nagumo gave the order
to have everything re-equipped for an attack

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:44.879
on Midway, and that meant swapping torpedoes
for bombs.

00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:48.809
The switch was in mid-completion when they
got word American ships had been spotted by

00:05:48.809 --> 00:05:52.060
scouts, and suddenly that meant they needed
to swap back to torpedoes again.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.420
What followed was more than chaos: Japanese
aircraft were slow to get into the air, and

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.180
ordnance was left scattered across the decks
of the carriers.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:04.000
All that ordnance was highly explosive, and
added fuel to the fire American dive-bombers

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.080
started dropping.

